   production-faithful config.)
PASS  B4.rebind allowed regardless of the resolved address (no 403; codes={'200': 79}, 79 succeeded against the permitted answer)
  note: 0 of 12 resolver replies put 127.0.0.1 first, so this run did exercise the
        loopback answer. Any 200/503 from it is an ALLOWED CONNECT to a loopback destination.

================================================================================
FINDING — CURRENT ARCHITECTURE, CONFIRMED VULNERABILITY

  Squid in CURRENT has NO destination validation. Of the SSRF corpus sent
  through the real HAProxy CONNECT listener (172.28.0.10:38888):

    names ALLOWED (no 403)     : 17
    names REFUSED with 403     : 0
    names with no reply at all : 8 (timeouts; Squid answered nothing)

  'Allowed' includes loopback, RFC1918, link-local and the cloud instance-
  metadata address 169.254.169.254, and it is not a timeout artefact: the
  baseline above served the legitimate endpoint on the same path, and the
  control above shows the ONE thing that is refused is a non-Safe PORT.

  This is production's real configuration reproduced end to end, not a POC
  artefact, and it is why TARGET's resolved-address policy is net-new
  security capability rather than a re-implementation of an existing control.

  Full write-up: docs/adr/0028-production-config-fidelity.md
  Stack-free reproduction of the ACLs themselves: tests/fidelity/run.sh
  (SQUID_CONF=squid.conf for this run; set SQUID_CONF=squid.conf.hardened
   to see the counterfactual refusal instead.)
